      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Ruptured heterotopic pregnancy as a cause of acute abdomen is not commonly seen. The aim of this case report is to stress the importance of sonographic examination of the pelvic region in all symptomatic patients in the first trimester of pregnancy."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHOD",
          "content": "The case report of a 23-year-old female with a ruptured heterotopic pregnancy is presented together with a brief review of the literature."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "A 23 year old female presented with signs and symptoms of an acute abdomen. She gave a history of 8 weeks amenorrhoea. Ultrasound scan (USS) showed an intra uterine pregnancy coexisting with a tubal pregnancy."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "All patients with symptoms of abdominal pain and history of amenorrhoea should have a sonographic examination of the pelvic region."
        }
      ]},
